From: lain3d <eyeofsteiner@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] usb: typec: mux: ps883x: seed the cached orientation from
 the hardware

retimer->orientation comes from devm_kzalloc(), so it starts as
TYPEC_ORIENTATION_NONE even when firmware has already configured the retimer
and it is passing traffic. The first orientation notification therefore always
compares unequal, and ps883x_sw_set() goes on to touch
REG_USB_PORT_CONN_STATUS_0 on a live chip.

On a machine that boots from a USB-C disk, that chip is in the path of the root
filesystem. The link drops roughly 16ms later with

  usb 4-1: cmd cmplt err -71
  sd 0:0:0:0: [sda] FAILED Result: hostbyte=DID_NO_CONNECT

and ext4 remounts read-only before the device recovers. Two independent
failures measured 16.1ms and 16.3ms after the notification, which is what makes
this a reaction to the driver rather than a link event.

Read CONN_STATUS_0_ORIENTATION_REVERSED at probe when the connection is already
present, and seed the cached value from it. Nothing changes for a retimer that
firmware did not configure - that path still takes the reset branch, where the
orientation is genuinely unknown.

This is the same defect as the qmpphy_mode seeding in
phy-qcom-qmp-combo (patch 3) and the unconditional reset in this driver's probe
(patch 2): cached driver state initialised from a default instead of read back
from hardware the firmware left running.

The dev_info() in ps883x_sw_set() is diagnostic and would be dropped before
submission.

Signed-off-by: lain3d <eyeofsteiner@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/mux/ps883x.c
@@ -310,6 +310,11 @@

 	guard(mutex)(&retimer->lock);

+	dev_info(&retimer->client->dev, "sw_set: cached=%d notified=%d -> %s\n",
+		 retimer->orientation, orientation,
+		 retimer->orientation == orientation ?
+		 "no change, not touching the chip" : "updating conn_status_0");
+
 	if (retimer->orientation != orientation) {
 		retimer->orientation = orientation;

@@ -466,9 +471,27 @@
 		 configured == 1 ? "leaving retimer running" : "resetting retimer");

 	if (configured == 1) {
+		unsigned int st0;
+
 		gpiod_direction_output(retimer->reset_gpio, 0);

 		/*
+		 * Seed the cached orientation from what firmware left in the
+		 * chip. Without this it stays at the zero devm_kzalloc gave it,
+		 * TYPEC_ORIENTATION_NONE, and the first orientation
+		 * notification looks like a change even when nothing changed -
+		 * so ps883x_sw_set() reaches for a live retimer's
+		 * REG_USB_PORT_CONN_STATUS_0 and the link drops ~16ms later.
+		 */
+		if (!regmap_read(retimer->regmap, REG_USB_PORT_CONN_STATUS_0, &st0)) {
+			retimer->orientation = (st0 & CONN_STATUS_0_ORIENTATION_REVERSED) ?
+					       TYPEC_ORIENTATION_REVERSE :
+					       TYPEC_ORIENTATION_NORMAL;
+			dev_info(dev, "probe: conn_status_0=%02x -> seeding orientation=%d\n",
+				 st0, retimer->orientation);
+		}
+
+		/*
 		 * Firmware left this retimer configured and passing traffic, so
 		 * leave it running. Do NOT fall through to the reset below: on a
 		 * machine that boots from a USB-C disk that traffic is the root
